WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.048
This is the executive
briefing for Book 00 of the

3
00:00:04.048 --> 00:00:08.074
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Orientation and Program.

4
00:00:08.074 --> 00:00:12.860
It runs about thirty minutes, and it's
for the people who sponsor, fund and

5
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:18.375
answer for modernization: executive
sponsors, CIOs, CISOs and program executives.

6
00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:19.563
One point up front.

7
00:00:19.563 --> 00:00:23.799
The workbook is independent technical
guidance, not federal policy.

8
00:00:23.799 --> 00:00:28.162
Where it says a law or directive
requires something, it cites the source.

9
00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:32.852
The phases, gates, principles and
decision rights are the workbook's own

10
00:00:32.852 --> 00:00:37.938
recommendation: one way to meet those
requirements, not a requirement itself.

11
00:00:37.938 --> 00:00:39.363
The argument is short.

12
00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:43.899
For twenty years Active Directory
quietly did governance nobody wrote down.

13
00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:49.163
Federal policy and vendor support dates
now set clocks the estate can't ignore.

14
00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:53.848
Meeting them one product at a time
rebuilds the same gaps somewhere new,

15
00:00:53.848 --> 00:00:57.263
so modernization has to run
as one governed program.

16
00:00:57.263 --> 00:01:01.951
I'll cover why now, what the program
is, who decides, and how it runs.

17
00:01:01.951 --> 00:01:06.500
Every decision slide names the decision,
its owner, and when it's needed.

18
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.100
Let's start with why.

19
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:09.888
Start with why.

20
00:01:09.888 --> 00:01:14.184
Most federal estates still run
the client/server model they built

21
00:01:14.184 --> 00:01:18.325
in the early 2000s, with an Active
Directory forest at the center.

22
00:01:18.325 --> 00:01:21.407
Placing a user or a computer
in the tree decided,

23
00:01:21.407 --> 00:01:25.200
all at once and with no separate
record, who administers it,

24
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.678
which Group Policy applies,
what its groups can reach,

25
00:01:28.678 --> 00:01:32.313
how it's named in DNS, and
which certificates it receives.

26
00:01:32.313 --> 00:01:35.987
Agencies got governance as a side
effect of running a directory.

27
00:01:35.987 --> 00:01:38.388
The cost is that it
was never written down.

28
00:01:38.388 --> 00:01:40.175
The cloud doesn't inherit it.

29
00:01:40.175 --> 00:01:43.225
Entra ID's administrative
units can't be nested,

30
00:01:43.225 --> 00:01:46.745
and Intune applies policy to
groups with no hierarchy,

31
00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:51.750
where two policies that disagree produce
a conflict, not an inheritance rule.

32
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:55.700
So every replacement needs someone to
decide what the old placement meant.

33
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.837
Retire the forest without
recovering those decisions,

34
00:01:58.837 --> 00:02:01.088
and you don't modernize your governance.

35
00:02:01.088 --> 00:02:02.088
You delete it.

36
00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:05.981
The first decision is the sponsor's,
when the charter is signed:

37
00:02:05.981 --> 00:02:10.638
treat this as governance reconstruction,
not only a technical migration.

38
00:02:10.638 --> 00:02:13.300
Next, the dates that make it urgent.

39
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:18.750
The second reason is the schedule, and
the first set of clocks is federal policy.

40
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:24.300
M-22-09 set Zero Trust objectives
for the end of fiscal 2024.

41
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:28.000
That date has passed, but
the actions remain in force.

42
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.434
CISA's BOD 25-01 set mandatory
cloud tenant configurations,

43
00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:37.387
with the policies due June 20, 2025.

44
00:02:37.387 --> 00:02:42.245
BOD 26-02 directs agencies
to remove internet-facing

45
00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:47.875
end-of-support edge devices, the
listed ones by February 5, 2027.

46
00:02:47.875 --> 00:02:54.728
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules for 2026
take mandatory effect on January 1, 2027,

47
00:02:54.728 --> 00:03:01.687
and FedRAMP accepts no new Rev5 certification
applications after June 11, 2027.

48
00:03:01.687 --> 00:03:04.188
The one that needs
attention first is logging.

49
00:03:04.188 --> 00:03:09.995
OMB M-26-14 rescinded M-21-31,
and its deadlines count from

50
00:03:09.995 --> 00:03:16.938
CISA's Logging Reference Architecture,
published August 20, 2026.

51
00:03:16.938 --> 00:03:22.076
Counted from that date, the Agency Logging
Plan falls on November 18 of this year.

52
00:03:22.076 --> 00:03:26.075
That's the program's arithmetic,
not a date OMB published.

53
00:03:26.075 --> 00:03:29.899
So the nearest decision: the CISO's
office confirms those dates,

54
00:03:29.899 --> 00:03:31.775
starting with the logging plan.

55
00:03:31.775 --> 00:03:34.030
Next, the vendor clocks.

56
00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.392
The second set of clocks
is vendor support.

57
00:03:37.392 --> 00:03:42.639
After end of support there are no new
security updates and no assisted support,

58
00:03:42.639 --> 00:03:46.593
and for an estate built in the
early 2000s, the dates cluster.

59
00:03:46.593 --> 00:03:53.205
Windows 10 and Exchange Server 2016
and 2019 ended in October 2025.

60
00:03:53.205 --> 00:03:57.912
SQL Server 2016, SharePoint
Server 2016 and 2019,

61
00:03:57.912 --> 00:04:03.443
and the last Windows Server
2012 updates ended this year.

62
00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:06.926
The one to watch is Windows Server 2016,

63
00:04:06.926 --> 00:04:11.717
which ends on January 12,
2027, under three months away.

64
00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:15.692
It runs many of the domain
controllers built in the last decade.

65
00:04:15.692 --> 00:04:20.648
And Microsoft Identity Manager
2016 ends on January 9, 2029,

66
00:04:20.648 --> 00:04:26.717
so an agency syncing HR data through it
has a date, not an open-ended bridge.

67
00:04:26.717 --> 00:04:31.042
A vendor date isn't a federal mandate
on its own, but the two connect.

68
00:04:31.042 --> 00:04:36.996
BOD 26-02 turns end of support into a
directed action for edge devices, and every

69
00:04:36.996 --> 00:04:43.042
security plan promises flaw remediation
an unsupported product can't deliver.

70
00:04:43.042 --> 00:04:46.082
So running past end of
support is a decision for the

71
00:04:46.082 --> 00:04:49.267
authorizing official, who
signs the risk acceptance.

72
00:04:49.267 --> 00:04:51.867
The first falls due on January 12.

73
00:04:51.867 --> 00:04:54.400
Next, how to read these clocks.

74
00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.475
The clocks are often quoted for more
than they say, so read them exactly.

75
00:04:59.475 --> 00:05:05.163
FedRAMP certifies cloud services, and each
agency still authorizes its own systems.

76
00:05:05.163 --> 00:05:07.690
Moderate is now a certification class:

77
00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:10.850
Class C includes the
current Moderate baseline.

78
00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:14.812
No federal document requires
a Zero Trust maturity stage.

79
00:05:14.812 --> 00:05:21.250
And BOD 25-01 covers production cloud
tenants, not on-premises Active Directory.

80
00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:24.150
Nothing makes the estate
fail on a single date.

81
00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:28.136
It's surrounded by deadlines, each
binding a different part of it,

82
00:05:28.136 --> 00:05:32.950
and each assuming the agency knows what
it runs, who owns it and what governs it.

83
00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:35.238
That's why this can't
be a set of projects.

84
00:05:35.238 --> 00:05:38.676
Moving devices to Intune
needs the policy carried over;

85
00:05:38.676 --> 00:05:42.263
Conditional Access needs the
access groups carried over.

86
00:05:42.263 --> 00:05:46.638
Separate projects rediscover those
decisions differently, or not at all.

87
00:05:46.638 --> 00:05:49.633
A program can sequence the
work against the dates;

88
00:05:49.633 --> 00:05:52.263
a collection of projects
can only race them.

89
00:05:52.263 --> 00:05:57.495
So the decision for the sponsor and CIO,
before the budget lines are set: fund and

90
00:05:57.495 --> 00:05:59.991
govern this as one sequenced program,

91
00:05:59.991 --> 00:06:03.050
with one dependency chain
and one set of gates.

92
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:05.310
Next, what that program is.

93
00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.535
Now, what the program is.

94
00:06:07.535 --> 00:06:10.892
It starts with a short charter:
the page the sponsor signs,

95
00:06:10.892 --> 00:06:14.035
and the page every later
decision is checked against.

96
00:06:14.035 --> 00:06:18.998
Is this in scope, does it serve an
objective, does it move a success criterion?

97
00:06:18.998 --> 00:06:22.102
The charter names the costs
it sets out to remove:

98
00:06:22.102 --> 00:06:27.300
migration risk hidden in dependencies,
drift between intended and actual state,

99
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:32.111
weak evidence of change, and the expense
of reconciling fragmented stores.

100
00:06:32.111 --> 00:06:34.560
Against those it sets six objectives.

101
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:39.930
At their heart is one authoritative origin
for organizational structure and identity

102
00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.504
attributes, with every other copy derived,

103
00:06:42.504 --> 00:06:46.035
and governance rebuilt
explicitly instead of inherited.

104
00:06:46.035 --> 00:06:51.285
Change runs in controlled waves that close
with evidence, without rip-and-replace.

105
00:06:51.285 --> 00:06:52.935
The scope is deliberate.

106
00:06:52.935 --> 00:06:56.547
The program feeds compliance
frameworks rather than replacing them.

107
00:06:56.547 --> 00:07:01.123
Identity providers, networks and
device managers stay the enforcers.

108
00:07:01.123 --> 00:07:03.760
And the agency executes its own cutover.

109
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:09.008
The decision: the sponsor signs the charter
and puts names in its governance table,

110
00:07:09.008 --> 00:07:13.098
starting with the sponsor and the
steward, as Phase 0's entry work.

111
00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:16.390
Next, how the charter's
success gets tested.

112
00:07:17.140 --> 00:07:20.102
A sponsor can't sign off
an outcome with no test.

113
00:07:20.102 --> 00:07:24.153
So the charter turns each of its
six success criteria into a measure,

114
00:07:24.153 --> 00:07:27.553
the evidence that shows it, and
the gate where it's checked.

115
00:07:27.553 --> 00:07:28.853
A few examples.

116
00:07:28.853 --> 00:07:33.099
The current-state assessment
covers every forest in scope,

117
00:07:33.099 --> 00:07:36.740
with zero open priority-one
findings, checked at G0.

118
00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:42.953
Every attribute that drives access has
exactly one designated source, checked at G1.

119
00:07:42.953 --> 00:07:47.269
The pilot division clears G2 with
zero drift for a hold period;

120
00:07:47.269 --> 00:07:50.315
fourteen days is the illustrative example.

121
00:07:50.315 --> 00:07:55.052
And drift detection is itself watched:
a missed run is a halt condition.

122
00:07:55.052 --> 00:07:57.252
The numbers are illustrative defaults.

123
00:07:57.252 --> 00:08:02.016
The agency sets its own, records them,
and doesn't change them silently.

124
00:08:02.016 --> 00:08:04.765
And the criteria stop at
the pilot on purpose.

125
00:08:04.765 --> 00:08:09.263
The sixth criterion is the sponsor's
own: leadership can describe the path,

126
00:08:09.263 --> 00:08:13.540
the risks and the measures without
citing internal decision records.

127
00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:17.002
The sponsor answers yes to
each pre-start question by G0,

128
00:08:17.002 --> 00:08:20.765
and the steering committee
reviews the drift trend monthly.

129
00:08:20.765 --> 00:08:23.410
Next, the principles underneath.

130
00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.322
The charter's principles expand to fifteen,
but one idea runs under all of them.

131
00:08:29.322 --> 00:08:33.528
Keep the record of what should be true
apart from the systems that enforce it,

132
00:08:33.528 --> 00:08:36.598
and make every change prove
itself against that record.

133
00:08:36.598 --> 00:08:40.286
In practice, every claim has
one authoritative origin.

134
00:08:40.286 --> 00:08:45.135
Every change starts from that source,
carries an authoritative authorization,

135
00:08:45.135 --> 00:08:49.985
runs with just-in-time least privilege,
and closes with provenance and evidence.

136
00:08:49.985 --> 00:08:52.797
No closure counts unless
it carries its proof.

137
00:08:52.797 --> 00:08:55.273
And drift detection
re-checks on a schedule.

138
00:08:55.273 --> 00:08:57.885
One rule deserves an
executive's attention.

139
00:08:57.885 --> 00:09:02.527
The program replaces the organizational
unit (OU) tree with attributes,

140
00:09:02.527 --> 00:09:06.698
and a user with an empty attribute
falls out of the dynamic group,

141
00:09:06.698 --> 00:09:09.373
and so out of every
policy that targets it.

142
00:09:09.373 --> 00:09:12.323
Attribute completeness
is a security control.

143
00:09:12.323 --> 00:09:17.166
The decision here is the steward's, and
it comes before the first change is made:

144
00:09:17.166 --> 00:09:21.935
set the evidence and provenance
expectations every later change must meet.

145
00:09:21.935 --> 00:09:24.430
Next, the phases and gates.

146
00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:28.512
A plan earns its keep by saying
what has to be true before

147
00:09:28.512 --> 00:09:31.555
the next step starts, not by
putting dates on a chart.

148
00:09:31.555 --> 00:09:37.565
Book 01 sets seven phases and six gates:
pre-flight, foundation, a pilot division,

149
00:09:37.565 --> 00:09:42.374
general rollout, legacy retirement,
retirement of AD DS itself,

150
00:09:42.374 --> 00:09:45.980
and then steady state,
which runs indefinitely.

151
00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:48.668
The gates are owned in
proportion to what they end.

152
00:09:48.668 --> 00:09:51.092
The steward clears G0, G1 and G4,

153
00:09:51.092 --> 00:09:55.455
and co-signs the pilot gate
with the executive sponsor.

154
00:09:55.455 --> 00:09:58.505
The sponsor and steering
committee clear rollout.

155
00:09:58.505 --> 00:10:02.617
The sponsor and CIO clear
the retirement of AD DS.

156
00:10:02.617 --> 00:10:04.142
Here's the budget catch.

157
00:10:04.142 --> 00:10:10.045
The program's master plan sequences 48
milestones over an illustrative 52 weeks,

158
00:10:10.045 --> 00:10:13.305
but its cutover ends with
AD DS still running.

159
00:10:13.305 --> 00:10:16.646
And Book 01's 52-week
model assumes a mid-size,

160
00:10:16.646 --> 00:10:21.380
clean forest; large federal
estates typically run multi-year.

161
00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:26.037
So the decision for the sponsor
and CIO, when they accept the plan:

162
00:10:26.037 --> 00:10:30.167
budget the AD DS retirement
phase on top of those 52 weeks.

163
00:10:30.167 --> 00:10:33.450
Next, the workstreams inside the phases.

164
00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:40.238
Five workstreams carry the work: identity,
policy, services, compute and access.

165
00:10:40.238 --> 00:10:42.954
They run together, because
finishing one before

166
00:10:42.954 --> 00:10:45.571
starting the next roughly
doubles the program.

167
00:10:45.571 --> 00:10:46.867
They still have an order.

168
00:10:46.867 --> 00:10:50.183
Identity comes first,
because policy, access,

169
00:10:50.183 --> 00:10:54.502
devices and data all decide
based on who or what is asking.

170
00:10:54.502 --> 00:10:56.239
Policy follows identity.

171
00:10:56.239 --> 00:10:59.959
Services, naming and
addressing, can run in parallel.

172
00:10:59.959 --> 00:11:03.241
Compute comes mid to late,
and access comes later,

173
00:11:03.241 --> 00:11:06.326
because it consumes both
identity and policy.

174
00:11:06.326 --> 00:11:10.517
The master plan organized the same
work as five technology pillars,

175
00:11:10.517 --> 00:11:13.989
and that left two gaps: nobody
owned the question of which

176
00:11:13.989 --> 00:11:17.683
policy surface owns a setting,
and access had no pillar at all.

177
00:11:17.683 --> 00:11:21.523
Book 01 closes them, with a
security-engineering lead for

178
00:11:21.523 --> 00:11:25.462
policy and a joint identity
and security lead for access.

179
00:11:25.462 --> 00:11:30.628
Every week lost in assessment or
attribute design moves every later gate,

180
00:11:30.628 --> 00:11:34.618
and one dependency never
relaxes: PKI waits for identity.

181
00:11:34.618 --> 00:11:40.323
The decision, during Phase 0: name the five
workstream leads, including those two.

182
00:11:40.323 --> 00:11:41.926
Next, the projects.

183
00:11:42.520 --> 00:11:47.714
Seven projects turn the program's most
common gaps into executable work,

184
00:11:47.714 --> 00:11:50.570
each with its own sponsor, lead and pilot.

185
00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:56.054
They cover identity lifecycle, devices,
network and access, continuous monitoring

186
00:11:56.054 --> 00:12:01.220
and evidence, privileged access, data
platforms, and the software supply chain.

187
00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:03.220
The suggested order matters.

188
00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:08.502
Identity goes first, because everything
else consumes its attributes and owners;

189
00:12:08.502 --> 00:12:13.395
a device project started on unreliable
attributes produces policy bypass.

190
00:12:13.395 --> 00:12:17.922
Privileged access is chartered fifth
but isn't fifth in practice, because the

191
00:12:17.922 --> 00:12:22.520
Foundation gate already needs emergency
access accounts vaulted and tested.

192
00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:26.370
Every charter asks for a working
pilot, not a finished estate.

193
00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:28.820
Two rules keep the portfolio coherent.

194
00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:31.589
No project designates
a second authoritative

195
00:12:31.589 --> 00:12:34.145
source for something another project owns.

196
00:12:34.145 --> 00:12:39.158
And every project emits evidence in the
shape the monitoring project defines.

197
00:12:39.158 --> 00:12:41.808
The projects aren't a second
governance structure.

198
00:12:41.808 --> 00:12:44.642
No project clears a
program gate on its own,

199
00:12:44.642 --> 00:12:47.719
and every project lead
reports to the steward.

200
00:12:47.719 --> 00:12:49.660
Next, who decides.

201
00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:51.435
Who decides?

202
00:12:51.435 --> 00:12:54.574
The book's advice: name the
people allowed to say no,

203
00:12:54.574 --> 00:12:59.398
give each decision one of them, and make
the repository remember who said yes.

204
00:12:59.398 --> 00:13:04.152
The executive sponsor, with the CIO,
owns scope, funding and sequence,

205
00:13:04.152 --> 00:13:08.822
breaks ties, and decides when
Active Directory components retire.

206
00:13:08.822 --> 00:13:13.472
The CISO sets the control requirements
every target surface must meet.

207
00:13:13.472 --> 00:13:16.404
The authorizing official
is the only official

208
00:13:16.404 --> 00:13:18.960
who can accept security and privacy risk.

209
00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:23.497
The steward leads the program office
and owns the plan and the repository.

210
00:13:23.497 --> 00:13:27.036
HR decides what each
organizational attribute means.

211
00:13:27.036 --> 00:13:31.847
And workstream leads own their designs,
inside the CISO's requirements.

212
00:13:31.847 --> 00:13:34.922
Federal requirements already
name some of these people.

213
00:13:34.922 --> 00:13:38.915
M-22-09 required a zero
trust implementation lead,

214
00:13:38.915 --> 00:13:43.984
and M-19-17 requires an agency-wide
ICAM governance structure.

215
00:13:43.984 --> 00:13:48.286
So the decision for the sponsor and
CIO, in Phase 0: name the sponsor,

216
00:13:48.286 --> 00:13:50.915
the steward and the steering committee,

217
00:13:50.915 --> 00:13:54.897
and build them from those existing
roles, not alongside them.

218
00:13:54.897 --> 00:13:58.700
Next, three decisions the
plans put on the wrong desk.

219
00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.838
Most of what the program's plans
said about decisions holds.

220
00:14:02.838 --> 00:14:04.100
Three items don't.

221
00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.750
Residual risk first.

222
00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.500
The plans let the security lead
and the steward accept it jointly.

223
00:14:09.500 --> 00:14:13.850
Under the Risk Management Framework,
only the authorizing official can.

224
00:14:13.850 --> 00:14:19.019
The others prepare and recommend, and
the acceptance carries a justification,

225
00:14:19.019 --> 00:14:21.563
compensating controls and an end date.

226
00:14:21.563 --> 00:14:25.690
Second, budget and licenses
belong to the sponsor and CIO,

227
00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:29.026
who own funding; the
steward prepares the case.

228
00:14:29.026 --> 00:14:34.062
Third, the master plan made the steward
the sole signatory at every gate.

229
00:14:34.062 --> 00:14:37.185
Book 01 spreads the
gates across the steward,

230
00:14:37.185 --> 00:14:40.475
the sponsor, the steering
committee and the CIO.

231
00:14:40.475 --> 00:14:43.113
A few separations keep
the governance honest.

232
00:14:43.113 --> 00:14:47.625
The authorizing official is a government
official, and the decision can't

233
00:14:47.625 --> 00:14:52.137
be delegated: contractors can prepare
a risk acceptance, but can't sign one.

234
00:14:52.137 --> 00:14:54.862
And requesters don't
approve their own changes.

235
00:14:54.862 --> 00:14:58.213
So from G0 onward, every
risk acceptance comes from

236
00:14:58.213 --> 00:15:01.724
the authorizing official in
writing, with an end date.

237
00:15:01.724 --> 00:15:04.380
Next, the people and the rhythm.

238
00:15:05.130 --> 00:15:06.155
Who does the work?

239
00:15:06.155 --> 00:15:10.513
The master plan estimated the program
office and workstreams at about

240
00:15:10.513 --> 00:15:15.018
five and a half full-time equivalents
for an illustrative 52-week program.

241
00:15:15.018 --> 00:15:17.730
It's a planning model,
not a staffing standard.

242
00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:20.899
What matters more is what
the estimate leaves out:

243
00:15:20.899 --> 00:15:24.377
application owners for every
application that moves,

244
00:15:24.377 --> 00:15:29.168
help desk capacity for every wave, HR
and contractor-source owners, records

245
00:15:29.168 --> 00:15:34.192
and privacy, and the CISO's and authorizing
official's time for risk decisions.

246
00:15:34.192 --> 00:15:38.070
The program can't run without them,
so budget for them explicitly,

247
00:15:38.070 --> 00:15:40.631
and cross-train two people per workstream.

248
00:15:40.631 --> 00:15:42.055
The rhythm is simple.

249
00:15:42.055 --> 00:15:46.243
A weekly report rates each
workstream green, yellow or red.

250
00:15:46.243 --> 00:15:51.685
The steering committee meets every two
weeks, gate readiness is reviewed monthly,

251
00:15:51.685 --> 00:15:55.206
and each gate review ends in
go, no-go or conditional go.

252
00:15:55.206 --> 00:15:59.143
Two consecutive red weeks for
any workstream go to the sponsor.

253
00:15:59.143 --> 00:16:04.471
The decision for the sponsor and CIO: put
those people in the Phase 0 business case,

254
00:16:04.471 --> 00:16:09.005
alongside the licenses, and approve
the budget before design begins.

255
00:16:09.005 --> 00:16:11.430
Now, how the program runs.

256
00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:16.518
It starts with pre-flight, and one
rule: assessment before action.

257
00:16:16.518 --> 00:16:21.180
No migration work starts until the
whole forest is inventoried, classified

258
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:25.768
and committed to the repository, and
every plan is generated from that data.

259
00:16:25.768 --> 00:16:30.396
First, the program builds the place
where every decision gets written down:

260
00:16:30.396 --> 00:16:33.980
a platform host with one
authoritative, protected writer.

261
00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:37.594
It's a Tier 0 system, and a
branch protection the steward

262
00:16:37.594 --> 00:16:40.967
can quietly override is a
convention, not a control.

263
00:16:40.967 --> 00:16:43.680
Then it reads the estate, read-only first,

264
00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:49.354
and the phase delivers twenty-three artifacts,
fifteen of them committed before G0.

265
00:16:49.354 --> 00:16:53.335
G0 has three conditions: the
platform host is operational,

266
00:16:53.335 --> 00:16:57.730
the assessment is committed, and
no priority-one finding is open.

267
00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.650
Every finding is classed Critical, High,

268
00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:04.455
Medium or Low, and each class
has a gate that closes it.

269
00:17:04.455 --> 00:17:06.980
The decision point is
the Critical finding.

270
00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:12.401
It's remediated before G0, or formally
accepted by the authorizing official,

271
00:17:12.401 --> 00:17:15.155
and the steward clears G0 on that basis.

272
00:17:15.155 --> 00:17:17.710
Next, foundation and pilot.

273
00:17:18.460 --> 00:17:24.682
Phase 1, Foundation, generates the plans
from the committed assessment and designs

274
00:17:24.682 --> 00:17:28.560
every surface from them:
attributes, dynamic groups,

275
00:17:28.560 --> 00:17:32.347
Intune policy, Conditional
Access, sync, DNS and PKI.

276
00:17:32.347 --> 00:17:36.773
One planning note: group processing
is planned in hours, not minutes.

277
00:17:36.773 --> 00:17:40.059
The steward clears G1 when
the designs are approved,

278
00:17:40.059 --> 00:17:45.104
the baseline Conditional Access policies
are enforced after at least a week in

279
00:17:45.104 --> 00:17:49.307
report-only, the emergency access
accounts are vaulted and tested,

280
00:17:49.307 --> 00:17:52.823
the HR feed is live, and every
High finding is remediated.

281
00:17:52.823 --> 00:17:56.099
Phase 2 proves the whole
pattern on real people:

282
00:17:56.099 --> 00:18:00.298
one division, typically IT,
which tolerates disruption best.

283
00:18:00.298 --> 00:18:05.223
It's measured against eight criteria,
from sign-in success to help desk volume.

284
00:18:05.223 --> 00:18:08.900
Set the thresholds in the decision
log before the pilot opens,

285
00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.784
and judge them from the
systems, not from opinion.

286
00:18:11.784 --> 00:18:14.122
Every surface has a rollback trigger.

287
00:18:14.122 --> 00:18:19.630
G2 is signed jointly by the steward and
the executive sponsor: zero drift for the

288
00:18:19.630 --> 00:18:25.222
agreed hold period, every criterion met,
and no hybrid-joined PC without a cut date.

289
00:18:25.222 --> 00:18:27.810
Next, rollout and retirement.

290
00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:33.764
Phase 3 repeats the proven pattern in
waves until every division has moved,

291
00:18:33.764 --> 00:18:36.785
usually by region,
lowest-risk region first.

292
00:18:36.785 --> 00:18:39.710
Group Policy links stay
in place during a wave,

293
00:18:39.710 --> 00:18:43.173
and come off only after seven
days of clean compliance.

294
00:18:43.173 --> 00:18:46.049
The sponsor and steering
committee clear G3

295
00:18:46.049 --> 00:18:49.159
when every division has
passed its own exit gate.

296
00:18:49.159 --> 00:18:53.285
Then the work changes character:
these phases take old things away.

297
00:18:53.285 --> 00:18:59.353
Phase 4 retires legacy pieces such as AD
FS, DNS forwarders, Group Policy links and

298
00:18:59.353 --> 00:19:05.422
legacy servers, each through its own plan,
and only while its rollback still works.

299
00:19:05.422 --> 00:19:07.348
The steward clears G4.

300
00:19:07.348 --> 00:19:12.119
Phase 5 retires AD DS itself, which
the master plan never reached,

301
00:19:12.119 --> 00:19:16.710
or reduces it to a documented
Kerberos island with an end date.

302
00:19:16.710 --> 00:19:18.698
Two steps are easy to forget.

303
00:19:18.698 --> 00:19:20.535
Records come before hardware.

304
00:19:20.535 --> 00:19:24.625
And because domain controllers
store every account's password hash,

305
00:19:24.625 --> 00:19:27.423
retired hosts and their
storage are sanitized.

306
00:19:27.423 --> 00:19:32.449
The sponsor and CIO clear G5, and
decide when each component retires,

307
00:19:32.449 --> 00:19:35.410
after the records officer is consulted.

308
00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:37.450
Next, steady state.

309
00:19:38.200 --> 00:19:41.537
Phase 6, steady state, has no exit gate.

310
00:19:41.537 --> 00:19:46.526
The modernized estate runs under continuous
governance: drift detection daily,

311
00:19:46.526 --> 00:19:49.653
reassessment weekly, a
monthly compliance report,

312
00:19:49.653 --> 00:19:53.450
quarterly access reviews and
an annual architecture review.

313
00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:58.362
The finish line is zero drift findings
and signed evidence flowing to the SIEM.

314
00:19:58.362 --> 00:20:02.199
Every finding gets a severity,
and every severity a clock.

315
00:20:02.199 --> 00:20:05.236
The master plan's defaults
run from four hours for

316
00:20:05.236 --> 00:20:08.050
a Critical finding to
thirty days for a Low one.

317
00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:13.160
The agency sets its own values; what the
program insists on is that there are some,

318
00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:17.263
written into its continuous
monitoring strategy and measured.

319
00:20:17.263 --> 00:20:19.262
Success is measured twice.

320
00:20:19.262 --> 00:20:22.175
Delivery measures turn green gate by gate.

321
00:20:22.175 --> 00:20:26.487
Outcome measures are what operations
keeps green after the project ends.

322
00:20:26.487 --> 00:20:29.706
A program can meet every
delivery measure and still

323
00:20:29.706 --> 00:20:32.625
fail if the outcomes drift
once the team leaves.

324
00:20:32.625 --> 00:20:35.138
That's why the handover
is part of the scorecard.

325
00:20:35.138 --> 00:20:39.720
Before the team leaves, the steward
records who owns the repository,

326
00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.912
each drift class, each
severity and each review.

327
00:20:42.912 --> 00:20:44.740
Next, the risks.

328
00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:50.352
The risk register names what can sink the
program, starting with five program risks.

329
00:20:50.352 --> 00:20:52.352
Hidden dependencies get missed,

330
00:20:52.352 --> 00:20:56.352
so a structured assessment and
the pilot surface unknowns early.

331
00:20:56.352 --> 00:20:58.669
Competing sources of truth persist,

332
00:20:58.669 --> 00:21:01.990
so each object class gets
one authoritative origin.

333
00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:05.379
The organization resists
making authority explicit,

334
00:21:05.379 --> 00:21:08.015
so decision rights get
one decider per row.

335
00:21:08.015 --> 00:21:12.442
And tooling leads the design, which
is why the target model comes first:

336
00:21:12.442 --> 00:21:15.877
tools bought first automate
the old ambiguity faster.

337
00:21:15.877 --> 00:21:20.878
Beneath them sit twenty-five delivery risks,
and a few deserve executive attention.

338
00:21:20.878 --> 00:21:26.223
Legacy applications that depend on NTLM
can't be evaluated by Conditional Access,

339
00:21:26.223 --> 00:21:30.940
so each dependency becomes a finding
with an owner and a dated exception.

340
00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:34.000
Strong certificate mapping
isn't a future risk:

341
00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:36.590
domain controllers already enforce it.

342
00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:39.415
And governance can decay
once the team leaves.

343
00:21:39.415 --> 00:21:43.547
The register doesn't satisfy a
control, but it tells the agency

344
00:21:43.547 --> 00:21:48.003
when to update its security plan
and risk assessment: at every gate.

345
00:21:48.003 --> 00:21:52.298
The authorizing official accepts
residual risk, and security risk

346
00:21:52.298 --> 00:21:56.440
left open past a gate becomes a
plan of action and milestones item.

347
00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:59.400
Finally, the decisions in one place.

348
00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.850
So, the decisions, with their owners.

349
00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:06.426
The executive sponsor charters
the program as governance

350
00:22:06.426 --> 00:22:10.075
reconstruction and signs the
charter as Phase 0's entry work.

351
00:22:10.075 --> 00:22:15.527
The sponsor and CIO fund one sequenced
program, with the AD DS retirement phase

352
00:22:15.527 --> 00:22:21.063
and the people beyond the core team in
the budget, approved before design begins.

353
00:22:21.063 --> 00:22:24.602
And they build the steward and
steering committee from the

354
00:22:24.602 --> 00:22:28.575
zero trust lead and ICAM governance
body the agency already has.

355
00:22:28.575 --> 00:22:32.102
The CISO's office confirms
the M-26-14 dates,

356
00:22:32.102 --> 00:22:38.275
starting with the Agency Logging Plan,
due November 18 by the program's count.

357
00:22:38.275 --> 00:22:43.578
The authorizing official decides whether
anything runs past end of support,

358
00:22:43.578 --> 00:22:46.975
beginning with Windows
Server 2016 on January 12.

359
00:22:46.975 --> 00:22:51.485
The steward sets the evidence
expectations before the first change,

360
00:22:51.485 --> 00:22:55.757
clears G0 only when every Critical
finding is closed or accepted,

361
00:22:55.757 --> 00:23:00.188
and, with the sponsor, records the
pilot's thresholds before it opens.

362
00:23:00.188 --> 00:23:03.462
And the steward records the
handover before the team leaves.

363
00:23:03.462 --> 00:23:05.112
None of these is a purchase.

364
00:23:05.112 --> 00:23:08.106
The estate's governance
was never written down,

365
00:23:08.106 --> 00:23:12.557
the dates that bind it now belong to
others, and the only way to meet them

366
00:23:12.557 --> 00:23:17.775
without losing that governance is to recover
it deliberately, once, in one program.

367
00:23:17.775 --> 00:23:18.600
Thank you.
